## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a deadline. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
            return task.Result;
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` is long-running and must cooperate with cancellation:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Callers pass a `Func<CancellationToken, T>` and check the token:

```csharp
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return ComputeResult();
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, the work must *voluntarily* check the token. CPU-bound loops without checkpoints will run past the timeout. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always `using` — leaking it leaks timers. |
| **Blocking calls ignore the token** | `Thread.Sleep(ms)` won't wake up. Use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` instead. |
| **Uncooperative third-party code** | If you can't modify the work to check a token, move it to a child **process** and use `Process.Kill()`. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary (here, to convert to `TimeoutException`). |

### What Was Removed

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Replacement |
|----|-----|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` (via timeout) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | No equivalent needed — cancellation is cooperative |
| Manual `new Thread(...)` | `Task.Run` with token |
